{"ts": 1789778782.5655942, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I still don't clear the bar. This is the decisive question, so let me reason about it rather than just keep mining.\n\nI've now evaluated ~60 specs. The structure is clear and boring: the features are near-uncorrelated, each carries a small independent signal, and combination Sharpe \u2248 \u221a(sum of squared individual Sharpes). Check: \u221a(0.597\u00b2 + 0.426\u00b2 + 0.492\u00b2) = \u221a(0.356+0.182+0.242) = \u221a0.780 \u2248 0.883, versus the observed 0.880. The additive model predicts my best result almost exactly.\n\nThat has an important implication. There is no hidden interaction or special combination to discover \u2014 the ceiling for 3 features is set by picking the three largest individual Sharpes, which I've already done. Continuing to grind through the remaining ~9,000 triples would not find real structure; it would only find specs whose in-sample Sharpe is inflated by luck. Those are exactly the specs that fail out-of-sample, which is why the bar is search-adjusted and sits above what honest search can reach here.\n\nThe individual Sharpes also need shrinking. With n=5000, the SE on an annualized Sharpe is ~1/\u221a20 \u2248 0.22. Selecting the top 3 of 40 features (with signs, effectively 80 candidates) induces real selection bias. f1 at 0.597 is ~2.7 SE and likely largely real; f3 (0.426) and f27 (0.492) are ~2 SE and their estimates are biased upward by having been picked as winners. Shrinking each toward zero, plausible true values are maybe 0.45, 0.28, 0.33, giving a true combined Sharpe around \u221a(0.203+0.078+0.109) \u2248 0.63.\n\nLet me spend a little budget testing that selection-bias hypothesis directly, by building a triple from mid-tier features whose individual estimates are mostly noise. The additive model predicts ~0.5; if these mid-tier features are mostly noise, the true OOS will be far lower, confirming the estimates are inflated."}
